      "hints": [
        "Use the cuboid directions: $\\mathbf i$ is along $OA$, $\\mathbf j$ along $OD$, and $\\mathbf k$ along $OC$.",
        "Locate $E$ and $F$ from the edge lengths, then average their position vectors because $M$ is their midpoint.",
        "Express the resulting position vector in $\\mathbf i,\\mathbf j,\\mathbf k$ components."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Set up the coordinates of the cuboid vertices based on the given dimensions $OA = 3$ (along $\\mathbf{i}$), $OD = 2$ (along $\\mathbf{j}$), and $OC = 2$ (along $\\mathbf{k}$):\n$$E = (3, 2, 0) \\quad \\text{and} \\quad F = (3, 2, 2)$$",
        "Calculate the midpoint $M$ of edge $EF$:\n$$\\vec{OM} = \\dfrac{\\vec{OE} + \\vec{OF}}{2} = \\dfrac{(3\\mathbf{i} + 2\\mathbf{j}) + (3\\mathbf{i} + 2\\mathbf{j} + 2\\mathbf{k})}{2} = 3\\mathbf{i} + 2\\mathbf{j} + \\mathbf{k}$$",
        "State the position vector of $M$:\n$$\\vec{OM} = 3\\mathbf{i} + 2\\mathbf{j} + \\mathbf{k}$$"
      ],
      "checking": {
        "mode": "ai",
        "ai_rubric": {
          "Using Position Vectors": "Since $M$ is the midpoint of the two stated points, averaging the displayed three position-vector components gives $\\overrightarrow{OM}=3\\mathbf i+2\\mathbf j+\\mathbf k$."
        },
        "criteria_source": "mark_scheme_structured"
      }
